.TH GPSFS 4
.SH NAME
gpsfs \- GPS receiver as a file system
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gpsfs
[
.B -m
.I mountpoint
] [
.I command ...
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Gpsfs
reads the NMEA 0183 sentences a GPS receiver sends, assembles them into
fixes with
.IR nmea (2),
and serves the receiver's position as files, mounted by default at
.BR /mnt/gps .
A receiver is a byte stream: a serial port
.RI ( eia (3)),
a network feed of NMEA (a phone application serving it over TCP),
.IR gpsd ,
or a recorded log; and anything that has no byte stream, such as a
platform's location service, writes sentences to
.BR feed .
The
.I command
words, if given, are written to
.B ctl
once the files are mounted.
.SH FILESYSTEM
.TF satellites
.TP
.B ctl
read: the source command in use, or nothing.
write: one command.
A source command returns once the source is open, or fails with the
reason it could not be opened; the previous source is stopped either
way.
.RS
.TP
.BI source " path \fR[\fPbaud\fR]"
a file or serial port, read to its end.
If
.I baud
is given,
.BI b baud
is written to
.IB path ctl
first, as
.IR eia (3)
expects.
.TP
.BI source " net\fB!\fPhost\fB!\fPport"
a network connection, as
.IR dial (2)
makes, carrying NMEA.
.TP
.BI "source gpsd" " host"
.IR gpsd ,
at
.IR host ,
.IB host ! port ,
or a full dial address; port 2947 by default.
It is asked for NMEA with
.BR ?WATCH ;
its JSON lines are passed over.
.TP
.BI replay " path"
a recorded log, its sentences sent at the pace of their times (no gap
longer than five seconds).
.TP
.B stop
no source.
.RE
.TP
.B status
one line, attr=value:
.B state=fix
(the last fix is a position),
.B nofix
or
.BR nosource ;
.B link=none
.RB ( opening ,
.BR open ,
.B closed
when the source ended,
.BR error );
.BI source= address;
.BI sentences= n
and
.BI bad= n
counted since start;
.BI age= seconds
since the last position;
.BI error= reason
for the last source that failed, quoted as
.IR tokenize
(see
.IR sys-tokenize (2))
expects.
.TP
.B position
.I "latitude longitude"
in decimal degrees, north and east positive, six places.
A read is an error,
.BR "no fix" ,
when the receiver has no position.
.TP
.B fix
the last fix, one line of attr=value fields, each given only when the
receiver sent it:
.BR time
(RFC 3339, UTC),
.BR lat ,
.BR lon ,
.B alt
(metres above mean sea level),
.B speed
(metres a second over the ground),
.B course
(degrees true),
.B mode
.RB ( none ,
.BR 2d ,
.BR 3d ),
.B q
(how it was found:
.BR gps ,
.BR dgps ,
.BR pps ,
.BR rtk ,
.BR float ,
.B dr
for dead reckoning,
.BR manual ,
.BR sim ),
.B sats
(satellites used),
.BR hdop ,
.BR vdop ,
.BR pdop ,
.B err
and
.B verr
(horizontal and vertical error estimates, metres, one sigma).
With no fix the line is the time and
.BR mode=none .
.TP
.B event
each read waits for the next fix and gives it, as
.B fix
would, or the fixes since the last read.
Every open has its own; a reader that falls far behind loses the
oldest.
A fix is given once the receiver has sent all it sends for one time,
or a sentence for the next time has come; a lost fix is told once.
The first fix from a new source is given at its first position
sentence, before the receiver is known to send others.
.TP
.B satellites
one line a satellite in view:
.I "system prn elevation azimuth snr used"
where
.I system
is
.BR gps ,
.BR sbas ,
.BR glonass ,
.BR galileo ,
.BR beidou ,
.BR qzss ,
.B navic
or
.BR gnss ;
.B -
stands for a value not known (an
.I snr
for a satellite not tracked), and
.I used
is 1 for a satellite in the fix.
.TP
.B nmea
the sentences, as received, one a line, from the open on; each read
waits for more.
Sentences that fail their checksum are not passed on.
.TP
.B feed
write-only: sentences written here are taken as the receiver's.
A write with a sentence that is not one (no
.BR $ ,
a bad checksum) fails, naming the fault; the others in it are taken.
.SH CAPABILITY MODEL
Following the receiver and changing what it says are separate files,
so what a process may do is what is bound in its name space:
.BR position ,
.BR fix ,
.BR event ,
.B satellites
and
.B nmea
let it follow the receiver;
.B ctl
and
.B feed
let it choose the source or supply a position.
A process that should only know where it is gets the first five.
.PP
.I Gpsfs
opens sources in the name space it was started in.
A serial port can always be named in the kernel's own syntax,
.BR #t/eia0 .
.SH EXAMPLES
A USB receiver on Linux, which
.IR eia (3)
finds after the
.B ttyS
ports:
.IP
.EX
; cat '#t/eia4status'
opens 0 ferr 0 oerr 0 baud 9600 ... dev /dev/ttyACM0
; gpsfs source '#t/eia4' 9600
; cat /mnt/gps/position
40.784000 -74.416667
.EE
.PP
A phone serving NMEA on port 11123, and a moving map's loop:
.IP
.EX
; echo source tcp!phone!11123 > /mnt/gps/ctl
; cat /mnt/gps/event
time=2026-10-31T14:32:05Z lat=40.784000 lon=-74.416667 alt=76.0 ...
.EE
.PP
A day's drive, again:
.IP
.EX
; cat /mnt/gps/nmea > /usr/$user/track.nmea &
\&...
; echo replay /usr/$user/track.nmea > /mnt/gps/ctl
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/gpsfs.b
.br
.B /appl/lib/nmea.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR nmea (2),
.IR eia (3)
.SH BUGS
One source at a time.
A source that ends or fails is not reopened; write the command again.
.PP
A phone's own location service is not yet a source: it needs a device
in the emulator for each platform, which would supply
.BR feed .
